This program runs the selftest available with the linux kernel source.
Compile and run the tests available at /tools/testing/selftests

KSM Test Documentation :-

  Supported <test type>:
  -M (page merging)
  -Z (zero pages merging)
  -N (merging of pages in different NUMA nodes)
  -U (page unmerging)
  -C evaluate the time required to break COW of merged pages.
  -P evaluate merging time and speed.
     For this test, the size of duplicated memory area (in MiB) must be
     provided using -s option.
  -H evaluate merging time and speed of area allocated mostly with huge
     pages. For this test, the size of duplicated memory area(in MiB)
     must be provided using -s option

kernel/kselftest.py.data/ksmtest.yaml contains YAML parameters that can
be used to control ksm_tests behaviour. The following parameters are to
be added under mm component. 
1. subtest     -> To specify ksm_tests
2. test_type   -> Kself supported test types.
3. Size        -> Size of duplicated memory area(in MiB) must be provided using -s option.
4. Dup_MM_Area -> Size of duplicated memory area (in MiB).

To run ksm_tests with -P and -H test types, following section can be added to
the YAML file
    mm:
        comp: "mm"
        subtest: "ksm_tests"
        test_type: "-H"
        Size: "-s"
        Dup_MM_Area: "100"
Note: The value of Dup_MM_Area depends on the use case.

To run with  -M, -Z, -N, -U and -C test types, there is no need to pass the
"Size" and "Dup_MM_Area" parameters.
Example YAML section:
    mm:
        comp: "mm"
        subtest: "ksm_tests"
        test_type: "-M"
        Size: ""
        Dup_MM_Area: ""

Following are few sample YAML inputs that can be used to control KSM and
other kselftests

To run only ksm_tests with upstream code:

component: !mux
    mm:
        comp: "mm"
        subtest: "ksm_tests"
        test_type: "-H"
        Size: "-s"
        Dup_MM_Area: "100"
run_type: !mux
    upstream:
        type: 'upstream'
        location: "https://github.com/torvalds/linux/archive/master.zip"

To run only a subset of powerpc specific selftests (alignment):

component: !mux
    power:
        comp: "powerpc"
        subtest: 'alignment'
        kself_args: "summary=1"
run_type: !mux
    upstream:
        type: 'upstream'
        location: "https://github.com/torvalds/linux/archive/master.zip"

To run both ksm_tests and alignment powerpc selftest:

component: !mux
    power:
        comp: "powerpc"
        subtest: "alignment"
        kself_args: "summary=1"
    mm:
        comp: "mm"
        subtest: "ksm_tests"
        test_type: "-H"
        Size: "-s"
        Dup_MM_Area: "100"
run_type: !mux
    upstream:
        type: 'upstream'
        location: "https://github.com/torvalds/linux/archive/master.zip"

To run entire powerpc specific selftests and ksm_tests:

component: !mux
    power:
        comp: "powerpc"
        kself_args: "summary=1"
    mm:
        comp: "mm"
        subtest: "ksm_tests"
        test_type: "-H"
        Size: "-s"
        Dup_MM_Area: "100"
run_type: !mux
    upstream:
        type: 'upstream'
        location: "https://github.com/torvalds/linux/archive/master.zip"

IOMMUFD Test Documentation :-

  Runs the iommufd kernel selftests from tools/testing/selftests/iommu.

  This test must run as root.

  The test is cancelled early on AMD CPUs if kernel logs do not contain
  "AMD-Vi" meaning iommu is disabled.

  Running kernel must have the following enabled:
  - CONFIG_IOMMUFD
  - CONFIG_IOMMUFD_TEST
  - CONFIG_VFIO_DEVICE_CDEV
  - CONFIG_AMD_IOMMU_IOMMUFD (AMD platforms only)

  Before running, the test reserves hugepages (default 512 pages of the
  system default hugepage size) so hugetlb-related subtests can pass.

kernel/kselftest.py.data/kselftest_iommu.yaml contains YAML parameters for
iommufd. The following parameters apply under the iommu component:
1. comp         -> Must be "iommu"
2. subtest      -> Selftest binary to run (default: iommufd). Use "all" for
                   make -C iommu run_tests, or "iommufd_fail_nth" for the
                   fault-injection binary.
3. nr_hugepages -> Number of hugepages to reserve (default: 512)
4. type         -> Must be "upstream"
5. location     -> URL of the kernel source archive or git repository
6. kself_args   -> Extra arguments passed to make run_tests (when subtest is
                   "all")

Issues :-

  Known issue (under debug): With mem_encrypt=on (AMD SME),
  iommufd_viommu.mock_viommu.viommu_alloc_with_data fails.

To run iommufd selftests with upstream code:

component: !mux
    iommufd:
        comp: "iommu"
run_type: !mux
    upstream:
        type: 'upstream'
        location: "https://github.com/torvalds/linux/archive/master.zip"

To run the full iommu selftest suite via make run_tests:

component: !mux
    iommu_all:
        comp: "iommu"
        subtest: "all"
run_type: !mux
    upstream:
        type: 'upstream'
        location: "https://github.com/torvalds/linux/archive/master.zip"

To run iommufd with a custom hugepage count:

component: !mux
    iommufd:
        comp: "iommu"
        nr_hugepages: 512
run_type: !mux
    upstream:
        type: 'upstream'
        location: "https://github.com/torvalds/linux/archive/master.zip"

VFIO Test Documentation :-

  Runs the vfio kernel selftests from tools/testing/selftests/vfio.
  Binds the given PCI device to vfio-pci before the run and restores it in
  tearDown.

  This test must run as root.

  The test is cancelled early on AMD CPUs if kernel logs do not contain
  "AMD-Vi" meaning iommu is disabled.

  Before running, the test reserves hugepages (default 512 pages of the system
  default hugepage size) for hugetlb subtests. tearDown restores the prior
  nr_hugepages value. If dmesg does not report interrupt remapping enabled
  (e.g. intremap=off), it also sets allow_unsafe_interrupts=Y for iommufd and
  for vfio_iommu_type1 (type1 path). Under that same interrupt-remapping
  condition, tearDown sets allow_unsafe_interrupts=N on both module parameters.

  Running kernel must have the following enabled:
  - CONFIG_VFIO
  - CONFIG_VFIO_PCI
  - CONFIG_IOMMUFD
  - CONFIG_VFIO_DEVICE_CDEV
  - CONFIG_AMD_IOMMU_IOMMUFD (AMD platforms only)

kernel/kselftest.py.data/kselftest_vfio.yaml contains YAML parameters for
vfio. The following parameters apply under the vfio component:
1. comp         -> Must be "vfio"
2. pci_device   -> PCI BDF (segment:bus:dev.fn)
3. subtest      -> Selftest binary (default: vfio_iommufd_smoke_test). Use
                   "all" for make -C vfio run_tests.
4. nr_hugepages -> Number of hugepages to reserve (default: 512)
5. type         -> Must be "upstream"
6. location     -> URL of the kernel source archive or git repository
7. kself_args   -> Extra arguments passed to make run_tests (when subtest is
                   "all")

Issues :-

  When running the full vfio selftest suite (subtest "all"), the upstream test
  vfio_pci_irq_test.msi.enable_trigger_disable may fail on some NVMe
  controllers (for example Non-Volatile memory controller: Samsung Electronics
  Co Ltd NVMe SSD Controller SM981/PM981/PM983).

To run vfio smoke test with upstream code:

component: !mux
    vfio_smoke:
        comp: "vfio"
        pci_device: "0000:03:00.0"
run_type: !mux
    upstream:
        type: 'upstream'
        location: "https://github.com/torvalds/linux/archive/master.zip"
